                         THE NEW MODULAR METHOD

Overview
--------

The new modular method solves several outstanding issues with the modular method
in IP Pascal. These were:

1. Isolation. Several modules used "coins", or prefixes, to make sure that they
don't conflict with user names. Examples include "sc_" and "ps_" for the syslib
and paslib modules, respectively.

2. "Zipper effect". Because modules need the support of other modules, those
lower modules would get pulled in by the used modules, and so forth. The result
is that an innocent module gets a lot of unrelated support module definitions
that it may not even need.

3. "loop locking". This is the effect where two mutual referencing modules lock
out each others definitions. For example, mod a uses mod b, which again uses
mod a. If mod a is parsed, it effectively suspends processing a to read b,
which cannot get the definitions from a, since it is suspended.

Previous method
---------------

The previous module method was based on the properties of the symbol table. 
Symbols are placed in lists according to a hash header, and trailed out from the
header. Each symbol has a level number it is associated with, and these levels
appear in the symbol table with the lowest level at the back, and the highest
level at the front. This means that global searches, searches of symbols in all
nesting of blocks, are found by looking through the symbols lists regardless of
the level number. Local searches, searchs of just the current block, search only
while the level number matches the current level, and stop when that is not
found.

The levels divided into:

0: system definitions (like "text", "integer", etc.).
1: module definitions
2-N: procedure/function definitions.

The current module, as well as all used modules, reside in the module level.
The definitions for the module being compiled, as well as the modules being 
used, are placed into the same level. This works because IP Pascal requires all
used modules to have distinct names. Where necessary, external flags are placed,
in procedure, function, variable and fixed entries. Otherwise, there is no
difference between a locally defined symbol and a used module one.

New method
----------

The new method separates symbol tables up by module. The same hash headers are
used, but they are encapsulated within modtrk records. Searches travel down 
symbols chains, but "leap" the gap between modules. This is done by keeping the
same hash index, then simply skipping to the next module when the end of the
chain is reached. Levels can span module blocks, and in fact this must occur by
definition, because all uses modules are resident in the same level 1.

There are a few rules that make a series of linked modules not the same as the
old unified symbols list:

1. Delayed defintions never cross module boundaries.

2. Overloads never cross module boundaries.

This allows certain routines to only address the current module when, say, 
ripping a symbols list out of the table.

The main idea of the new module blocks system is that it allows a list of uses
blocks to be assembled to process a particular module, then rearranged for the
next. In this way, a used module gets only the visibility of modules it
specifically asks for in its uses list.

Because of the rearrangement requirement, there are  mltety records that exist
only to make flexible lists of modules. They are created and distroyed as needed
to create a specific list for a particular module. There are two stacks that
contain the current module lists, the uselst and joinlst. The uselst is the main
entity we refer to in the program parse, and it replaces the old unified symbols
table.

When a module is read in, it is created at the top of the logical modules stack.
Then its definitions are filled out, and it may be removed and inserted into
other modules at will.

A module starts by creating a new module entry, and creating a new list entry
it and pushing that onto the uses stack. This will create a uselst consisting of
the system module, with the new module ontop of it.

When the new module processes a uses or joins, it removes itself from the top of
the uselst, then the paruses routine is called. For each module to be used or
joined, both a new module entry is created, and a new list entry stacked to
the uselst. This will create a series of modules stacked onto the uselst, in
order of their appearance in the uses list. If the module is in the joins list,
the only difference is that the module is moved from the top of the uses list to
the top of the joins list, and all of the modules in its uses list are removed
from uselst.

When a module process is complete, it will have the following arrangement on
uselst:

1. system module.
2. Uses modules.
3. Current module.

This means that it will virtually have the same symbols table as the old system,
except that it is divided up into modules.

MODULE REUSE

Because a given module can come and go, as it is referenced by submodules, we
have a system to remove and reinsert such modules into the uses list. When it
needs to be removed, it is simply delinked, and the list entry discarded. This
is because the module entry still exists, only the list reference to it is
discarded.

When a module is reinserted to the uses list, a new list entry record is created
for it, and it is inserted. Then, it is checked for conflicts with the existing
uses list. This consists of checking for duplicates in the entire uses deck. 
This process is time consuming, but it can be reduced by keeping track of what
modules were checked with what others, and not performing this work again.
